Small fish in a big pond.

Xbox and Microsoft were in a weird place in 2011. Apple and Microsoft had essentially flipped market positions. Apple’s hardware and software integration was sophisticated and they were innovating in services and even retail stores. Xbox 360 was a huge success, and it had pioneered online services, but it was getting long in the tooth and it needed a successor.

At the same time, unknown to me, Microsoft had decided to shutter it’s innovation studio called Pioneer Studios. It was located in Pioneer Square, one of the oldest part of Seattle, in a cool renovated brick building. They had created the Zune mp3 player, and were creating another device called Courier; a foldable dual-screen touch tablet with a stylus.

Pioneer studios was over-budget, and after reviewing their progress on Courier, they pulled the plug. In addition, they were closing the studio in Seattle and everyone was expected to come back to the mothership in Redmond.

All of this collided in Studio A in Redmond. A team of super-talented, opinionated product designers from Pioneer Studios with an established team of Xbox designers, PM’s and dev’s who had their own codebase not even connected to Windows at the time. Everyone was scrambling for control while leadership jockeyed for power positions. It was an uncertain, anxious time and one of the worst I’ve spent at a company.

My initial hiring manager left after three months. Within 18 months more than half of the people left. My mentor who got me hired left after the first year citing it as toxic and not workable. It was just too many people trying to decide what they should make. It took until well into 2013 for the dust to settle and teams to start to trust and work together again.

Once things settled it was a fantastic job. At it’s best it provided a safe place to explore ideas, collaborate and build a design discipline. My first team built out SmartGlass, the second-screen companion app to Xbox One.

